WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.900
Welcome to First Principles Daily, episode 33.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:07.580
Let's run the Magic Wand number and the idiot index on today's subject.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:16.080
Messages cross the Atlantic in two weeks by ship until engineers treated the ocean floor itself as the medium and moved a word in minutes.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:26.760
Before the electric telegraph, any word sent from London to New York traveled inside a mail pouch on a steam packet that required roughly 14 days to complete the crossing.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:32.640
That 14-day figure already baked in favorable winds and good coal consumption.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:40.600
In bad weather, the same voyage could stretch longer, yet the fundamental limit stayed fixed by the hull moving through water.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.660
The binding limit was the physical speed of the hull through water, not the information itself.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:55.440
No amount of clever packing inside the pouch could change how fast the packet displaced sea water or how long it took to cover 3,000 kilometers.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:07.360
Engineers who asked what physics actually required for moving a signal instead of a letter replaced the ship with a copper conductor insulated by gutta perchah and laid across the seabed.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:16.380
Once the conductor sat on the ocean floor, the electrical impulse no longer needed to push a hull through waves or wait for coal to be shoveled.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:22.980
The time for a message dropped from weeks to minutes and the cost structure of moving information changed for good.

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:31.580
For centuries, the only proven method of moving a written message across an ocean was to place it aboard a vessel and wait for the vessel to arrive.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:40.160
Ship owners and merchants optimized hull shape, sail plans, and later steam engines, yet the fundamental constraint remained unchanged.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:45.000
The letter had to share the same physical journey as every other ton of cargo.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.940
A typical transatlantic crossing in the 1840s still required 12 to 16 days depending on weather and season.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:02:00.100
That range already reflected the best average performance after decades of incremental sail and engine improvements.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:05.280
The lower end of 12 days was still measured in full days, not hours.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:15.120
The cost of that journey included the ship's capital, crew wages, coal or canvas, insurance, and the opportunity cost of capital tied up for the duration.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:25.660
Because every message incurred the full marginal cost of the voyage, senders accepted that a business letter or diplomatic dispatch would be days or weeks old on arrival.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:32.620
No one questioned the premise that information must travel inside a container that also carried passengers and freight.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.300
The container was simply made faster and more reliable.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:47.800
Newspapers printed European news only after the latest packet docked, and currency traders in New York adjusted exchange rates only after the same vessel's mail reached Wall Street.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:57.460
The entire commercial and political rhythm of the Atlantic world was therefore paced by the sailing schedule rather than by the intrinsic speed of an electrical impulse.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:03.080
That schedule was treated as an immutable fact of geography and engineering practice.

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:11.700
One objection sometimes raised is that faster clipper ships or more efficient steam packets could have narrowed the gap without new technology.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:16.520
In practice, those improvements still left the fundamental physics untouched.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:25.260
Any vessel had to displace water and contend with wind, current, and storm, so the time floor remained measured in days rather than minutes.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:40.120
Another objection is that optical signaling from short towers or semaphore chains might have bridged short distances faster, yet those systems stopped at the water's edge and offered no path across 3000 kilometers of open ocean.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:52.060
The ship therefore remained the unchallenged default because every incremental gain in hull speed or reliability still preserved the original analogy that a message is simply another piece of freight.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:04:05.160
The first principle step was to separate the signal from any physical container and ask what minimum material and energy would be required to propagate an electrical impulse across 3000 kilometers of seawater.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:20.280
A rough magic wand estimate begins with the conductor itself. Several thousand tons of copper whose commodity value at mid 19th century prices amounted to perhaps a few hundred thousand pounds sterling for the entire cable.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:31.280
To see the scale, picture roughly 3000 tons of copper priced at roughly 100 pounds per ton. That arithmetic alone lands near 300,000 pounds.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:37.020
The dominant insulation material, gutta percha, added another comparable sum.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:45.260
Adding armor wires, tar, and the cost of the laying ship still left the raw material floor well below 1 million pounds.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:59.020
The first Atlantic cables, however, were contracted at roughly 1 million pounds each. That contract price implies an idiot index on the order of 2 to 3 when measured against bulk commodity prices alone.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:12.900
To see the ratio clearly, consider the materials alone. Copper at a few hundred thousand pounds plus a similar amount for gutta percha and the rest of the basic components puts the floor somewhere under 1 million pounds.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:20.320
Divide the 1 million pound contract price by that material floor and the result sits between 2 and 3.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:32.260
The index signals that the dominant expenses lay in manufacturing uniformity, electrical performance, and the repeated failures of early lays rather than in the copper or gutta percha themselves.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:37.800
The decisive engineering moves followed directly from the physics of signal loss.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:47.900
Gutta percha was selected because it remained flexible and non-conducting after prolonged immersion, unlike earlier rubber compounds that absorbed water.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:59.960
The cable was built with a central copper core surrounded by several layers of gutta percha, then wrapped in hemp and iron armor wires to survive the laying process and seabed abrasion.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:08.920
Early cables failed because the insulation was too thin or because the conductor resistance was too high for the voltage that could be applied without breakdown.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:17.800
Each failure revealed a specific limit that the next design iteration addressed by increasing insulation thickness or conductor cross section.

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:26.900
The 1858 cable carried a detectable signal but degraded within weeks because its gutta percha layer was insufficient to prevent leakage.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:44.440
The 1866 cable used the thicker dielectric and a larger copper section, allowing reliable operation for years, laying the cable required solving the additional problem of paying out 3000 kilometers of heavy rope under controlled tension from a moving ship.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:54.800
The steam ship Great Eastern was rebuilt with enormous cable tanks and linear braking engines so that the cable could be released at a speed matched to the ship's progress.

00:06:54.800 --> 00:07:02.760
Each of these steps attacked a concrete physical bottleneck rather than copying the practices of overland telegraph lines.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:10.780
A common objection is that simply scaling up landline techniques, thicker wire and more battery voltage should have sufficed.

00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:22.780
In reality, the seawater itself acted as a parallel conductive path that drained the signal unless the dielectric was thick enough and uniform enough to block leakage over the entire length.

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:39.260
Another objection is that the high idiot index reflected only temporary teething problems of a new technology, yet the cost structure remained dominated by the need to achieve electrical continuity and mechanical survival in a single continuous deployment.

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:48.320
Once those constraints were met, later cables reused the same core design with only incremental refinements in armor and payout machinery.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:59.920
Once the 1866 cable entered commercial service, a message that had previously taken 14 days could be transmitted in roughly 10 minutes of electrical transit time.

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:12.400
Manual encoding and decoding added further minutes at each end. The cost per word fell from the equivalent of several days' wages for a courier to a few shillings once traffic volume rose.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:21.000
Stock exchanges in London and New York began to move in near real time and newspapers could print same day foreign news.

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:29.540
The idiot index of the cable itself dropped as manufacturing yields improved and as subsequent cables reused proven designs.

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:38.520
Yet the absolute cost of each new cable remained high because the laying operations still required a specialized ship and a multi-month expedition.

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:52.680
Early cables also suffered from low bandwidth. The electrical characteristics of the long conductor limited signaling speed to a few words per minute until loading coils and later electronic repeaters were introduced.

00:08:53.520 --> 00:09:00.500
Those limits were accepted because the alternative continued reliance on ships remained slower by orders of magnitude.

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:13.760
A cable whose price was driven mainly by the need to survive a single high risk deployment rather than by the copper inside it revealed that the expensive step was not the conductor but the process of placing it.

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:20.840
The same principle applies whenever a new physical path must be created before information or material can flow.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:26.360
The first workable path carries the full cost of proving that path is possible.

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:34.880
Tomorrow the show returns with another concrete case in which someone measured the raw material floor and then removed everything that stood above it.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:42.160
What single constraint would have to be removed next for trans-oceanic communication to approach the speed of light itself?

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:45.080
That's first principles daily for today.

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:52.040
If this changed how you see what things cost share it with someone who'd appreciate it and subscribe wherever you listen.

